SUMMARY:Book Talk: Power Lines: Maps That Shaped the Way We See the World by Peter Keating
DESCRIPTION:All maps show us where we are. Some maps also try to tell us 
 what to think—and that’s where things get really interesting. In Power 
 Lines: Maps That Shaped the Way We See the World (Black Dog & Leventhal\, 
 2026)\, award-winning journalist Peter Keating presents a collection of 
 maps that have defined borders\, influenced social policy\, and enacted 
 political\nchange. In Power Lines\, Keating argues that the most dramatic 
 and influential of all maps are political maps. Rather than reflecting 
 geographical reality\, political maps express man-made boundaries\, 
 territorial claims\, and national identity. All the maps in Power Lines 
 share two qualities: historical significance and persuasive power. Travel 
 to ancient Greece to define existence\; to the age of Exploration to 
 express borders\, identities\, and ambitions\; to antebellum America to 
 track the ideas that paved the path to the Civil War\; to the 1884 Berlin 
 Conference to see Western leaders carve up Africa. See the map of Adolf 
 Hitler’s speaking engagements in 1933 that looks like a rock concert tour 
 poster. Maps of gerrymandering. Of redlining. Of military targets and of 
 peace treaties. The map of the world—distortions and all—that hangs on 
 thousands of classroom walls. And the polarizing red-and-blue 
 election\nresults maps that Americans are confronted with every two years. 
 From dominion to exploration and national definition to partitions to 
 social and ideological movements to persuasion and war\, in striking images 
 and riveting stories that span continents and centuries\, Keating makes a 
 compelling case for why\, in the age of the internet\, the power of 
 political maps is unmatched\, and is as potent as ever.\n\nAbout the 
 Author\nPeter Keating is the author of Power Lines: Maps That Shaped the 
 Way We See the World (Black Dog & Leventhal\, 2026)\, which received a 
 starred review from Publishers Weekly. He is a narrative journalist whose 
 wide- ranging coverage of sports\, finance\, politics\, and history has 
 been featured in publications such as GQ\, Mother Jones\, National 
 Geographic\, and\nPolitico. He has written four national columns\, 
 including “The Biz” and “Numbers” for ESPN\, where he was part of 
 teams that won three National Magazine Awards. A 10-time winner of New York 
 Press Club awards\, Keating was a founding member of ESPN’s Investigative 
 Unit\, where his longform projects included pieces that exposed for the 
 first time how the NFL dealt with brain injuries. From 2022 to 2025\, he 
 co-hosted the Underdogs podcast. He has also written Dingers! A Short 
 History of the Long Ball (ESPN Books\, 2006). He lives in Montclair\, 
 N.J.\, with his wife Karen\, their daughters Ellie and Samantha\, and their 
 dog\, Otis. Follow him on X at @PeterKeatingNJ.
LOCATION:Montclair Main - Conference Room
ORGANIZER;CN="Montclair Library":MAILTO:events@montclairlibrary.org
CATEGORIES:Author Talk, Books, Film & Theater
UID:LibCal-10306-17076321
URL:https://bccls.libcal.com/event/17076321
END:VEVENT
