DTSTAMP:20261210T000000Z
SUMMARY:Washington's Crossing: 1776
DESCRIPTION:An America 250 Program\n\nHistorian Glenn LeBoeuf explains how 
 General Washington’s audacious bets at Trenton and Princeton salvaged the 
 freedom cause.\n\nAbout the program\n\nBy the winter of 1776\, 
 Washington’s army had shrunk from its high of almost 31\,000 to about 
 3\,500 in December. Many politicians were demanding Washington's 
 replacement\, but those who had stuck by him through the bitter defeats on 
 Long Island and Manhattan and the swift retreat across New Jersey to the 
 relative safety of Pennsylvania remained committed to the cause of 
 independence. Come for the stories of these heroic troops and the numerous 
 political\, logistical\, topographical\, and tactical problems they faced. 
 Above all\, come hear about the leader who inspired them: Washington's 
 soldiers would not quit this cause or this man. If you have never heard 
 Glenn speak\, strap in for a fantastic experience!\n\nAbout the 
 presenter\n\nSince 1984\, Glenn LeBoeuf has been lecturing in history and 
 biography\, with a particular emphasis on the personalities of George 
 Washington and Abraham Lincoln. His research has contributed to our 
 understanding of their leadership styles and the historical contexts in 
 which they operated\, and his teaching methods have inspired many students 
 to explore the complexities of American history.
LOCATION:West Orange - Community Room 1
ORGANIZER;CN="West Orange Library":MAILTO:events@westorange.bccls.org
CATEGORIES:Lecture
UID:LibCal-10341-16936546
URL:https://bccls.libcal.com/event/16936546
END:VEVENT
